doc:sec-48h-ss-3 a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "48H(3) Retention of seized things" ;
    values:kind "subsection" ;
    values:originalText "(3) The thing may be returned under subsection (2) either unconditionally or on such terms and conditions as the Secretary sees fit. Note: This section does not apply in relation to the thing if section 52AAB applies in relation to the thing: see subsection 52AAB(2)." ;
    cof:pageNumber "328"^^xsd:integer .

concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-48j a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "48J Issuing officer may permit a thing to be retained"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-48j ;
    values:originalText "(1) An authorised person may apply to an issuing officer for an order that he or she may retain the thing for a further period if: (a) before the end of 120 days after the seizure; or (b) before the end of a period previously specified in an order of an issuing officer under this section; proceedings in respect of which the thing may afford evidence have not commenced. (2) If the issuing officer is satisfied that it is necessary for an authorised person to continue to retain the thing: (a) for the purposes of an investigation as to whether an offence against this Act has been committed; or (b) to enable evidence of an offence against this Act to be secured for the purposes of a prosecution; or (c) for the purposes of an investigation as to whether a civil penalty provision has been contravened; or (d) to enable evidence of a contravention of a civil penalty provision to be secured for the purposes of civil proceedings; the issuing officer may order that an authorised person may retain the thing for a period (not being a period exceeding 3 years) specified in the order. (3) Before making the application, the authorised person must: (a) take reasonable steps to discover who has an interest in the retention of the thing; and  Chapter 6  Administration" ;
    skos:definition "(1) An authorised person may apply to an issuing officer for an order that he or she may retain the thing for a further period if: (a) before the end of 120 days after the seizure; or (b) before the end of a period previously specified in an order of an issuing officer under this section; proceedings in respect of which the thing may afford evidence have not commenced. (2) If the issuing officer is satisfied that it is necessary for an authorised person to continue to retain the thing: (a) for the purposes of an investigation as to whether an offence against this Act has been committed; or (b) to enable evidence of an offence against this Act to be secured for the purposes of a prosecution; or (c) for the purposes of an investigation as to whether a civil penalty provision has been contravened; or (d) to enable evidence of a contravention of a civil penalty provision to be secured for the purposes of civil proceedings; the issuing officer may order that an authorised person may retain the thing for a period (not being a period exceeding 3 years) specified in the order. (3) Before making the application, the authorised person must: (a) take reasonable steps to discover who has an interest in the retention of the thing; and  Chapter 6  Administration"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "5fe0e954b49b5aef392692e5c4a71733b91d302d9ff248a8fe5958504848bd8c" ;
    cof:pageNumber "329"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-48j-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-48j-ss-2 ;
